    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#gao"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gao.jpg" alt="Gao" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#gao">Gao</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tombeau_dAskia_in_Gao_by_David_Sessoms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Sessoms from Fribourg, Switzerland, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Songhai Empire was the largest empire in West African history, reaching its apogee in the late fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a>. Its roots, however, extend much further: Songhai-speaking peoples had occupied the bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-river">Niger River</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gao">Gao</a> since at least the ninth century CE, and a Songhai kingdom under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#za-dynasty">Za dynasty</a> (or Dia dynasty) had for a time been a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sonni-dynasty">Sonni dynasty</a>, which traditionally threw off Malian overlordship in the fourteenth century, produced the ruler who would build the empire: Sunni Ali (r. 1464–1492), one of history's most formidable military commanders, who launched the campaigns that transformed a modest Sahelian kingdom into a continental empire. His first great success was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#songhai-conquest-of-timbuktu">Songhai conquest of Timbuktu</a> in 1468, taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tuareg">Tuareg</a> who had held it since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a>'s decline; his second was the commercially vital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inner-niger-delta">inland delta</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a>, which, according to tradition, resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-djenne">Siege of Djenné</a> for seven years before submitting around 1473. These acquisitions gave Songhai control of the most important centres of trans-Saharan commerce and Islamic scholarship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>. Sunni Ali's death in 1492, reportedly by drowning while crossing a river in flood, was followed in 1493 by a coup in which Muhammad Ture, one of his leading commanders and a devout Muslim, defeated Sunni Ali's son Sunni Baru and took the title Askia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#askia-muhammads-pilgrimage">Askia Muhammad's pilgrimage</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mecca">Mecca</a> (1496–1498), during which the Abbasid shadow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a> recognised him as his deputy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#western-sudan">western Sudan</a>, gave the empire a religious legitimacy that Sunni Ali's syncretic rule had never achieved and inaugurated the empire's golden age.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="Timbuktu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donkeys,_Timbuktu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flickr user: Emilio Labrador Santiago de Chile https://www.flickr.com/people/3059349393/, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Songhai economy rested on three interlocking foundations: the agricultural productivity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inner-niger-delta">Niger Inland Delta</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gold-trade">trans-Saharan gold</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salt-trade">salt trade</a>, and the commercial revenues of the great cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gao">Gao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a>, that served as the empire's commercial and intellectual centres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-river">Niger</a>'s annual flood, which inundated the inland delta for several months each year, deposited rich alluvial soils that supported intensive cultivation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sorghum">sorghum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a>, generating surpluses sufficient to feed large urban populations and a substantial non-agricultural workforce. The gold trade, which carried gold from the goldfields to the south and west through Songhai territory to Moroccan and North African markets in exchange for salt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taghaza">Taghaza</a> mines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> luxury goods, generated large customs revenues for the imperial treasury. Timbuktu was the empire's showpiece: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>, the Granada-born traveller raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fez">Fez</a>, who visited around 1510, described a wealthy city where books were sold at greater profit than any other merchandise and where scholars gathered around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sankore-madrasah">Sankore</a> mosque. Royal estates worked by servile labour, many of them hereditary dependants of the crown, produced grain for the court and army. Long-distance trade was managed through an established commercial infrastructure of weights, measures and merchant networks, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wangara">Wangara</a> (Dyula) merchants serving as the principal long-distance intermediaries. The economy's fundamental vulnerability was its dependence on trans-Saharan routes that could be disrupted by political instability in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-al-mansur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur.jpg" alt="Ahmad al-Mansur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_al-Mansur_by_Andr%C3%A9_Thevet.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">André Thevet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Songhai military combined a professional core with large contingents drawn from subject peoples, and it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a>'s military genius that defined the empire's methods of expansion. Its most distinctive element was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-river">Niger River</a> fleet, a corps of war canoes crewed by specialists from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sorko">Sorko</a> fishing communities who had navigated the Niger for centuries. This amphibious capability gave Songhai a decisive advantage in the waterway geography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-bend">Niger Bend</a>, allowing rapid troop movement and river-borne supply that land-based opponents could not match. The cavalry, drawn from the Songhai nobility and allied peoples, provided the mobile striking power for campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahel">Sahel</a> and along the desert edge, while infantry from subject agricultural peoples supplied the mass needed for sieges. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Ali's campaigns were marked by extraordinary speed and political ruthlessness: his harsh treatment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>'s scholars, whom he regarded as politically subversive, produced a tradition of hostile chronicles that still colours the historical record against him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a> created a standing army of full-time soldiers and appointed military commanders such as the <em>balama</em>, while the <em>hi-koy</em> commanded the river fleet; he led campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mossi">Mossi</a> in 1498 and against Aïr and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hausa">Hausa states</a> in the early sixteenth century. The empire's military weakness was exposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Moroccan invasion of 1591</a>: the Sa'adian sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> sent an expeditionary force of around 4,000 men across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a>, armed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arquebus">arquebuses</a> and light <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">cannon</a> that the Songhai cavalry and infantry, however numerous, could not withstand. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> a much larger Songhai army was routed in a single engagement, demonstrating the asymmetry that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons could create between a small, well-equipped force and a numerically superior but conventionally armed army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#djenne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djenne.jpg" alt="Djenné" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moschee_von_Djenn%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">qiv, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Songhai government combined the administrative inheritance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> with innovations introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a> that gave the empire a more centralised character than its predecessor. The empire was divided into provinces under governors bearing titles such as <em>fari</em> or <em>farma</em>, often royal kinsmen or trusted commanders rather than local hereditary chiefs, the most senior being the <em>kurmina-fari</em> who governed the western provinces; this marked a departure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a>'s more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indirect-rule">indirect rule</a> and gave Songhai greater cohesion over its territorial core. Askia Muhammad maintained a court protocol of considerable formality, in which subjects prostrated themselves before the ruler and sprinkled dust on their heads, rituals that combined West African royal ceremony with elements of Islamic court tradition. The judiciary centred on Islamic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qadi">qadis</a></em> who administered Islamic law in the towns, alongside customary legal systems for the largely non-Muslim rural population, creating a dual legal framework that accommodated the empire's religious diversity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a> enjoyed distinct arrangements, governed through negotiation between imperial appointees and the Islamic scholarly and merchant elites whose cooperation was indispensable to commerce; in Timbuktu the <em>qadi</em> exercised wide authority. Timbuktu's scholarly community, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sankore-madrasah">Sankore</a> mosque and supported by imperial patronage, produced learning of international standing: the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-baba-al-timbukti">Ahmad Baba al-Timbukti</a>, whose works survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscript</a>, represents the tradition that made Timbuktu synonymous with scholarship across the Islamic world. The empire's chief administrative weakness was royal succession: after Askia Muhammad was deposed by his son Askia Musa in 1528, a series of violent successions destabilised the administration and diverted military resources to internal conflict, culminating in the civil war of 1588 that left the empire divided as the Moroccan threat developed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sahel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahel.jpg" alt="Sahel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sahel">Sahel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mud_Mosque_-_Bani_-_Sahel_Region_-_Burkina_Faso_-_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adam Jones, Ph.D., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Songhai Empire's historical significance rests primarily on its role as the high point of West African political and commercial development in the pre-colonial era, demonstrating the capacity of African societies to build large, administratively complex states integrating diverse populations across vast territories. Its control of the trans-Saharan trade at a period of intense activity connected West African gold and other goods to North African, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and Middle Eastern markets: West African gold circulated through the Ottoman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluk</a> and European monetary systems, shaping commercial conditions across the connected world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>'s intellectual culture, preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> many of which survive in private family libraries in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a>, represents one of the most substantial bodies of pre-colonial African scholarship, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, mathematics, medicine and history; the great Timbuktu chronicles, the <em>Tarikh al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sudan">Sudan</a></em> and <em>Tarikh al-Fattash</em>, written in the seventeenth century, preserve the memory of Songhai itself. The empire's administration of diverse peoples established precedents that influenced the Moroccan-controlled pashalik of Timbuktu that succeeded it and later states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#western-sudan">western Sudan</a>. The Moroccan destruction of the Songhai state in 1591 was, from a broad historical perspective, a severe interruption of West African political development: the Moroccan occupation failed to establish an effective replacement administration, trans-Saharan trade was disrupted, and the political fragmentation that followed prevented the emergence of a comparable large-scale state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-bend">Niger Bend</a> for generations. Insecurity and the breakdown of order along the river damaged the agricultural systems that had made the region productive, with lasting demographic consequences for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahel">Sahel</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sahara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahara.jpg" alt="Sahara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sahara_real_color.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Project Apollo Archive, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Songhai Empire's collapse was sudden and externally imposed, among the most dramatic destructions of a major African empire in history. The precipitating cause was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Morocco's invasion of 1591</a>, driven by the Sa'adian sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a>'s determination to control the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gold-trade">trans-Saharan gold</a> trade, and timed to exploit the civil war that had divided Songhai since 1588. His expeditionary force of around 4,000 soldiers, largely Andalusian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moriscos">Morisco</a> and European renegade arquebusiers together with Moroccan troops, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a> under the command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#judar-pasha">Judar Pasha</a>, himself a Spanish-born convert, in one of the most audacious military operations in African history, losing a substantial part of its strength to thirst and heat on the march. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> on 13 March 1591, the Songhai army assembled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#askia-ishaq-ii">Askia Ishaq II</a>, estimated variously at between about 12,500 and 40,000 men, was routed by sustained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arquebus">arquebus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">cannon</a> fire; a herd of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> that the Songhai reportedly drove towards the Moroccan lines stampeded back into their own ranks, and the Songhai cavalry, unable to close with their opponents under concentrated fire, disintegrated. The Moroccans occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gao">Gao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> soon afterwards. What followed was not orderly replacement but devastation: the Moroccan administration could not govern the vast Songhai territories effectively, the gold it expected to seize proved largely out of reach in distant goldfields, and trans-Saharan trade suffered from the insecurity created by the conquest. The Songhai royal family retreated south-east and maintained an independent state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dendi">Dendi</a> for generations, but the integrated commercial and administrative system that had made the empire great was irrecoverably shattered. By the early seventeenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-bend">Niger Bend</a> had fragmented into competing polities, with the descendants of the Moroccan soldiers, the Arma, ruling Timbuktu, and the region's population and economic output would not recover to pre-conquest levels for generations.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Songhai Empire stands as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>'s greatest pre-colonial political achievement, a state that demonstrated the full sophistication of African governance, commerce and scholarship, and whose sudden destruction by a small Moroccan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gunpowder">gunpowder</a> army in 1591 represents one of history's most consequential technological asymmetries.</em></p>

